WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:20.199
I am Sue Desmond-Hellman. I am Chancellor at the University of California, San Francisco,

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.399
and I want to tell you about the drug development story

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.866
for Herceptin. Let me start by telling you

00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:31.399
about my background. I am a medical oncologist by training.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:35.632
After medical school I came to the University of California, San Francisco

00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:40.766
and did my training in internal medicine and then medical oncology.

00:00:40.767 --> 00:00:44.266
and had the great fortune during the last couple of years

00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:49.932
as a faculty member in the late 80s of spending time in Kampala, Uganda

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:53.599
as the leader of the adult cancer center,

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.766
caring for patients with Kaposi's sarcoma.

00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:59.866
And a lot of my early thinking about product development

00:00:59.867 --> 00:01:04.966
actually came from the time I spent in Uganda caring for patients with Kaposi's sarcoma

00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:08.166
and a real quest and a passion to make life better for patients.

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:13.299
In the mid 90s I came to San Francisco again

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:17.166
to join Genentech, the first biotech company,

00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:19.999
and I had a lot of wonderful experiences in product development at Genentech.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.999
And one of those great experiences is the story that I

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.932
want to tell you about today.

00:01:25.933 --> 00:01:29.899
So let's talk about some big picture things about

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:31.199
great drug development.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.632
And when I say drug development, I mean the entire process

00:01:34.633 --> 00:01:38.532
of bringing an idea or a concept from the labs

00:01:38.533 --> 00:01:43.132
into the clinic and then through the approval process

00:01:43.133 --> 00:01:45.999
so that the medicine can be broadly available for patients.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.432
First and foremost, and you can't do great drug development without it,

00:01:50.433 --> 00:01:54.599
you need a very deep understanding of the basic science.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.766
What's the hypothesis that you are testing?

00:01:56.767 --> 00:01:58.732
What's the question you are asking?

00:01:58.733 --> 00:02:00.799
And does that question make sense

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.099
given the basic science and the fundamentals behind what you are trying to prove?

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.766
The characteristics of the drug are important. If the drug doesn't get to the target,

00:02:09.767 --> 00:02:12.866
or it doesn't stay long enough in the circulation,

00:02:12.867 --> 00:02:16.966
the half life, then you can't improve the outcome for that patient.

00:02:16.967 --> 00:02:20.999
Targeting the right patients, or personalizing the therapy

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.899
is the modern way to do drug development, and in part

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:26.932
it's the modern way to do drug development based on experiences

00:02:26.933 --> 00:02:28.832
like the one we had with Herceptin.

00:02:28.833 --> 00:02:34.799
Setting a high bar in the clinic isn't something everyone does.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.066
The risk of setting a high bar in the clinic

00:02:36.067 --> 00:02:38.199
is that you can fail.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.799
The benefit of setting a high bar in the clinic

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.066
is that if you succeed, it matters a lot.

00:02:44.067 --> 00:02:48.132
It makes a big difference. People need to re-write the textbooks.

00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:52.132
Think again about what is possible for those patients.

00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:57.966
So setting a high bar in the clinic is the most inspirational way to do great drug development.

00:02:57.967 --> 00:03:01.232
And what I actually like most about great drug development is

00:03:01.233 --> 00:03:03.699
you don't do it alone. You have to work

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:06.699
effectively with colleagues in a collaborative way.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:11.499
and you particularly have to work effectively with key regulatory decision makers.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.966
In America, that's the Food and Drug Administration,

00:03:14.967 --> 00:03:17.099
but there are similar bodies all over the world,

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.032
and you have to work effectively with those regulators

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:23.266
so you can do the clinical trials, and ultimately,

00:03:23.267 --> 00:03:26.199
so you can have broad applicability of your new product.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:32.266
So let me start the Herceptin story with telling you about the scientific rationale

00:03:32.267 --> 00:03:36.999
in breast cancer. And this story really rests on

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.566
some critical information about the role of oncogenes in driving cancer.

00:03:41.567 --> 00:03:47.599
And this specific oncogene, Human Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor 2,

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:52.432
or HER2, is a gene that was thought to be associated with

00:03:52.433 --> 00:03:55.499
accelerating the growth of breast cancer.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:59.066
And the first clinical description of that growth acceleration

00:03:59.067 --> 00:04:01.799
came from Dennis Slamon and his colleagues as published in

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:05.699
Science in 1987, and it is shown on this slide.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:11.499
If patients had too much HER2, either amplification or overexpression of HER2,

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:16.099
as measured by FISH or immunohistochemistry,

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.732
that was associated with a shorter survival.

00:04:19.733 --> 00:04:24.266
So about 25% of women in this trial were

00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:30.732
HER2 positive, or over-expressing, and their median survival was only 3 years.

00:04:30.733 --> 00:04:33.999
Whereas the HER2 normal, about 75% of women

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.232
who had a normal amount of HER2 lived a median of 6 to 7 years.

00:04:39.233 --> 00:04:42.732
That's a dramatic difference, and that matters in

00:04:42.733 --> 00:04:46.466
the clinic. That had a big impact, when I was a practicing oncologist

00:04:46.467 --> 00:04:49.899
on how I thought about the outcomes in those patients.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:54.632
So what that meant for a patient was that she could only expect to work half as long

00:04:54.633 --> 00:04:58.366
as her fellow breast cancer patients who were HER2 normal.

00:04:58.367 --> 00:05:02.066
At the time this study was done it was particularly troubling to see this outcome

00:05:02.067 --> 00:05:06.366
because we had no remedy for this HER2 positive status in the patient.

00:05:06.367 --> 00:05:10.232
So this description was scientifically compelling,

00:05:10.233 --> 00:05:14.899
but in terms of drug development it also outlined for us an unmet need.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:18.032
A difficult situation for patients that potentially

00:05:18.033 --> 00:05:22.466
we could fix.  So here is the molecule that allowed

00:05:22.467 --> 00:05:26.699
us to think about addressing this HER2 positive

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:29.899
or HER2 over-expressing breast cancer.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:33.632
And this is the picture or a cartoon showing

00:05:33.633 --> 00:05:39.266
Trastuzumab. Trastuzumab is the USAN name, or what some people call the

00:05:39.267 --> 00:05:41.632
generic name for Herceptin.

00:05:41.633 --> 00:05:44.199
I am going to use Herceptin because it less of a mouthful.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.099
But this is the monoclonal antibody.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.799
And monoclonal antibodies, at the time that Herceptin was developed

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:55.632
were thought to be kind of a pie in the sky dream for a smart bullet,

00:05:55.633 --> 00:06:00.866
a guided missile targeted to certain things like HER2

00:06:00.867 --> 00:06:02.799
that were expressed on cancer cells.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.499
And the beauty of Herceptin was that this monoclonal antibody

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:11.466
was humanized. Most of the antibody, greater than 90%

00:06:11.467 --> 00:06:16.099
was human. Less than 10%, and in fact in this case, 7%

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:20.666
was murine. And that meant that unlike some of the early antibodies

00:06:20.667 --> 00:06:24.366
human beings didn't reject the antibody as foreign.

00:06:24.367 --> 00:06:28.832
So Herceptin was a humanized monoclonal antibody

00:06:28.833 --> 00:06:32.232
that specifically targeted HER2,

00:06:32.233 --> 00:06:36.032
allowing us to test the hypothesis, if we direct an antibody

00:06:36.033 --> 00:06:43.332
against HER2 can we improve outcomes in women with HER2 positive breast cancer.

00:06:43.333 --> 00:06:48.466
So this is a slide that shows you the development timeline for Herceptin.

00:06:48.467 --> 00:06:50.999
And a couple points I want to make on this slide,

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.732
you notice the slide is a very long timeline.

00:06:53.733 --> 00:06:56.499
It takes a lot of time to do great drug development.

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:01.299
and that is because each step along the way you ask and answer

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:04.266
specific questions. Now the first study

00:07:04.267 --> 00:07:07.799
of Herceptin was all the way back in 1991.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:11.032
And that is when the investigational new drug

00:07:11.033 --> 00:07:15.099
or IND was filed to ask the FDA, we want to do clinical trials

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:18.199
with this new concept for patients in the clinic.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:23.532
By 1994, the phase 2 studies were being done

00:07:23.533 --> 00:07:27.132
based on an early rating that this was safe enough to give patients.

00:07:27.133 --> 00:07:33.132
In phase 2 you ask, is this medicine going to help patients?

00:07:33.133 --> 00:07:36.332
Does it look promising? And you start to figure out the dose

00:07:36.333 --> 00:07:39.666
for which you are going to do the most important phase of testing,

00:07:39.667 --> 00:07:42.832
or Phase 3, which opened up in 1995.

00:07:42.833 --> 00:07:48.332
The other things that are shown in this slide as we get into the late 90s

00:07:48.333 --> 00:07:52.866
and then into the 2000s are a series of steps with the regulators.

00:07:52.867 --> 00:07:56.766
With the US regulators in terms of filing

00:07:56.767 --> 00:07:59.599
a BLA or a biologic license application,

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:04.299
and with the ex-US regulators and what this shows is that something

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:07.432
had to be done that is special with a targeted therapy

00:08:07.433 --> 00:08:11.299
and that special thing is filing both Herceptin,

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:14.132
or Trastuzumab approval, and HercepTest.

00:08:14.133 --> 00:08:17.832
This makes drug development even more challenging.

00:08:17.833 --> 00:08:21.566
You have to ask for approval of your product,

00:08:21.567 --> 00:08:27.066
Herceptin, and simultaneously ask for approval for your diagnostic, HercepTest.

00:08:27.067 --> 00:08:30.466
These were both concurrently approved in 1998

00:08:30.467 --> 00:08:35.999
in the metastatic setting or breast cancer that has spread.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:42.866
And then not until 2005 did FDA approve Herceptin in the adjuvant setting,

00:08:42.867 --> 00:08:47.266
or following primary surgery as adjunctive to standard chemotherapy.

00:08:47.267 --> 00:08:53.132
Now the HER2 testing part of the Herceptin

00:08:53.133 --> 00:08:56.932
experience was a critical part of the lessons that I learned

00:08:56.933 --> 00:08:59.499
about product development when I worked on Herceptin.

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:02.932
And what this slide shows you is a great example

00:09:02.933 --> 00:09:06.099
of why a diagnostic matters so much.

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:09.166
It actually makes drug development cheaper

00:09:09.167 --> 00:09:12.999
and faster. And why is that? Well, this is a

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:18.132
statistical representation of what might have been with the Herceptin study.

00:09:18.133 --> 00:09:21.666
In patients whose breast cancer has spread,

00:09:21.667 --> 00:09:23.532
metastatic breast cancer, or MBC,

00:09:23.533 --> 00:09:28.299
we knew before  Herceptin median survival was 22 months.

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:32.166
So 50% of patients had died by 22 months

00:09:32.167 --> 00:09:35.632
when you studied first line metastatic breast cancer.

00:09:35.633 --> 00:09:39.766
Let's say that we expected the benefit of Herceptin to be to extend that

00:09:39.767 --> 00:09:43.499
survival from 22 months to 27 months,

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:46.199
or a 5 month improvement in survival.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:49.966
Well if everyone in the study, or 100% of the patients,

00:09:49.967 --> 00:09:53.899
were HER2 positive, that 5 month difference would

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:59.566
take about 1250 patients and 52 months duration of study.

00:09:59.567 --> 00:10:02.932
If we didn't have the diagnostic test,

00:10:02.933 --> 00:10:07.399
and the truth was that only 25% of our study population had

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:10.666
HER2 over expression, that study would have shown

00:10:10.667 --> 00:10:16.332
a 1.25 month difference, and taken 349 months,

00:10:16.333 --> 00:10:20.032
and 11,000 patients. We couldn't have done that study.

00:10:20.033 --> 00:10:21.366
That study wasn't possible.

00:10:21.367 --> 00:10:23.899
So the diagnostic allowed for a smaller patient

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:26.399
group and a faster study.

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:29.399
And here is the answer to the question,

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:32.499
does Herceptin help women with metastatic breast cancer?

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:34.732
The great news is that the answer is yes.

00:10:34.733 --> 00:10:38.366
In fact, as we had predicted,

00:10:38.367 --> 00:10:41.599
the median survival extended from 20.3

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:46.432
months to 25.4 months, a 25% improvement

00:10:46.433 --> 00:10:50.366
in survival in women with metastatic breast cancer,

00:10:50.367 --> 00:10:53.499
all of whom were HER2 positive, when treated with Herceptin.

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:58.832
Even better news came when we studied Herceptin  early on

00:10:58.833 --> 00:11:00.966
in the adjuvant setting.  The time

00:11:00.967 --> 00:11:04.766
to first distant recurrence doubled.

00:11:04.767 --> 00:11:07.066
The time that women stayed in remission doubled

00:11:07.067 --> 00:11:10.399
when Herceptin was given just after surgery.

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:13.832
And this news was so dramatic that it got a standing ovation

00:11:13.833 --> 00:11:15.599
when it was presented for the first time.

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:19.999
In my experience the first time I have ever seen a standing O at a medical meeting.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.632
Great news for patients treated with Herceptin.

00:11:24.633 --> 00:11:26.766
Here is the New England Journal article

00:11:26.767 --> 00:11:30.166
that talked about that great news for women

00:11:30.167 --> 00:11:33.799
in the adjuvant setting, HER2 positive, treated with Herceptin.

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:36.666
And the most important thing that I want to point out

00:11:36.667 --> 00:11:38.566
in this New England Journal article is

00:11:38.567 --> 00:11:42.666
the last line: "This will completely alter our approach

00:11:42.667 --> 00:11:44.832
to the treatment of breast cancer."

00:11:44.833 --> 00:11:48.299
Now you have to ask: is the patient HER2 positive?

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:51.999
Can you help women by targeting

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.566
this scientifically based new therapy

00:11:54.567 --> 00:11:57.566
at what could become the Achilles' heel of the cancer?

00:11:57.567 --> 00:11:58.832
And the answer was yes.

00:11:58.833 --> 00:12:03.132
So let me come back to the key things.

00:12:03.133 --> 00:12:06.099
We understood the science. We had the right drug.

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:10.366
We targeted the right patients, set a high bar (survival in the clinic)

00:12:10.367 --> 00:12:15.166
and we worked all the way along the line with Food and Drug Administration.

00:12:15.167 --> 00:12:18.699
And the best thing about working on Herceptin

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:21.399
and the experience that I had was that I know now

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.032
hundreds of thousands of patients

00:12:23.033 --> 00:12:25.499
are going to benefit from this work.

00:12:25.500 --> 01:00:00.000
And that's the best news of all in great drug development.

